  "source": "mcweed I-7 / U533-U541 (pin shoulder, then the wedge flare, jamming the fingers: the same miss twice) with mined4's 'sweep tests (motion)' -- 'swept the fingers two full turns through it', 'pin surface swept through two unscrew turns' -- and mined2 I-6, the pin that had to be strictly shorter than the funnel interior. Static fit checks missed the interference twice; only sweeping the real motion found it.",
  "summary": "Relieve three internal ribs for a lug that unscrews: 360 degrees of rotation COUPLED to 6 mm of rise. The lug starts in the gap between ribs, so the start pose is clear and any static check passes. The relief is a helical band -- each rib is notched at a different height (1, 3 and 5 mm up) and each notch is tilted by the rise across the rib's width. The natural wrong answer is a flat band at the lug's starting height, z -2.8..2.8: correct at t=0, it leaves rib standing where the risen lug must pass and removes rib that was never in the way. The lug's cross-section is spelled out in the prompt on purpose: a first run had a model read '4 wide' as an arc following the bore rather than a flat block, which is a defensible reading and moves the swept corners in by ~0.25, failing a root probe even though the motion reasoning was entirely right. The task measures the coupled turn-and-rise, so the cross-section is given rather than guessed.",
  "expects_question": false,
  "align": "none",
  "grid": 240,
